To improve the performance of the ASCII-art algorithm,
we implemented 2 caching mechanisms that avoid redundant
computations across multiple runs of the algorithm:
1. Caching Sub-Image Brightness Values:
    if the image and resolution remain unchanged between runs,
    the brightness values will remain the same.
    So, to avoid recomputing brightness values on subsequent runs, we store:
        double[][] prevBrightness;
        int prevResolution;
        Image prevImage;
    if all three match the current run, the algorithm reuses the cached brightness matrix
    instead of recalculating it.
2. Caching the Character Matcher
    The mapping from characters to their normalized brightness values is
    independent of the image itself. It depends only on the character set.
    To avoid rebuilding the matcher for the same charset, we store:
        char[] prevCharset;
        SubImgCharMatcher prevMatcher;
    If the current charset is identical to the previous one,
    we reuse the existing SubImgCharMatcher.
    Otherwise, we create a new matcher and update the cache.
Data Structure Choice for SubImgCharMatcher:
    We represent the brightness values of characters using two hash maps:
        private final Map<Character, Double> charBrightnessMap;
        private Map<Character, Double> normalizedCharBrightnessMap;
    This design choice is motivated by several considerations:
        1. O(1) Average-Time Access for Brightness Lookup
        2. Efficient Add/Remove of Characters
        3. Clear Separation Between Raw and Normalized Brightness
            We maintain two maps:
                charBrightnessMap that holds raw brightness values
                normalizedCharBrightnessMap that holds values re-scaled to [0, 1]
            This separation ensures:
                Raw brightness values never need to be recomputed
                Normalization can be recalculated efficiently whenever the charset changes
        4. Readable Matching Logic
        5. Extensibility - Easy to Extend with Additional Features
    Overall, using hash maps for brightness storage in SubImgCharMatcher
    provides a good balance of performance, flexibility, and code clarity,
    making it well-suited for the needs of the ASCII-art algorithm.